arm64: dts: h713: fit the board-mgr node to this board's fan, and enable it

The board_mgr node was copied from the vendor DTS of a board with a
PWM-controlled fan. On this one the fan is a plain 3-wire on/off load -- red
+V, black GND, yellow tach -- on the shared PB5 rail, established by bench
instrumentation in patch 0030. Three things had to go before the driver could
be switched on:

- PH17 was claimed twice in one node: as "pwms = <&pwm 0 40000 0>" (PWM
  channel 0 muxes out on PH17) and as "fg-gpio" for the tachometer. The
  driver really does devm_pwm_get(), so enabling it as-is would have muxed
  PH17 to an output and driven it against the fan's open-collector tach line
  -- destroying the only speed feedback the board has. All PWM properties are
  dropped: pwms, pwm-names, pwm_ids, pwm_period_ns, default-duty, and the
  temp-array/duty-array tables that feed them. "pwm-fan" leaves the
  compatible list with them; a pwm-fan node without pwms is not one.

- fan1 is dropped. It had duty tables and no tachometer; this board has one
  fan, and keeping the subnode would add a permanently-zero fan2_input.

- The "gpios" subnode is dropped. It mapped usb-power-gpio to PL3, but PL3 is
  cam-usb-power-gpio in the stock GPIO map -- the stock HY310 kernel DTS puts
  usb-power-gpio on PL5, a different rail. U-Boot already drives both PB5 and
  PL3 high by name in h713_poweron_lines(), so Linux has nothing to add here,
  and driving an unverified PL5 from the kernel is not a thing to do blind.
  The mapping wants resolving before this comes back.

fan0 gains "pulses-per-revolution = <2>": the driver counts rising edges only
(patch 0141), where the stock driver requests both edges and divides by 4.
Same fan, same RPM, counted once per pulse.

The temperature thresholds stay as the vendor set them (warn 55 C, poweroff
60 C), but the lradc-table behind the conversion is unverified on this
hardware, so the driver reports and does not act -- see patch 0141. That also
matches the stock kernel, which at this threshold escalates the fan mode and
sends a TEMP_STATE=SHUTDOWN uevent rather than calling kernel_power_off().

With that the node is switched on for the qz713df-a1 board (the DTB in the
FIT), so temperature and fan speed become readable under /sys/class/hwmon.
The stale hy310_board_mgr.no_rpm_shutdown=1 in the qz713-v2 bootargs goes too:
the module was never built, and 0141 removes that parameter.

